{
  "message": "Thanks — quick important note before we continue: I can’t look up accounts using only a phone number or date of birth. Our account lookup tools require either the exact full name on the account (case‑sensitive, include middle initial if present) or the email address on the account (or a Rho‑Bank user ID, if you know it).",
  "why": "This is a security and tooling limitation — once you give one of those identifiers I will: 1) pull your account record, 2) confirm the DOB (11/03/1990) and phone (312‑555‑0481) you provided, 3) log the verification with a timestamp, and 4) fetch your card transactions and rewards totals for investigation.",
  "options": [
    {
      "label": "Provide exact full name",
      "detail": "Give the full name exactly as it appears on the account (include middle initial if present). This is the fastest way if you can recall the exact formatting."
    },
    {
      "label": "Provide the email address later",
      "detail": "If you can access your email soon, provide the email and I’ll proceed then."
    },
    {
      "label": "Reconcile without account access (no verification)",
      "detail": "If you prefer not to wait, you can type a few recent transactions (date, merchant, amount, and the points shown) and I’ll walk through how the rounding/conversion rules apply to explain the discrepancy — this does not require verification."
    },
    {
      "label": "Transfer to a human agent",
      "detail": "If you want human help now and can’t provide name or email, I can transfer you. Tell me if you’d like that and I’ll initiate the transfer."
    }
  ],
  "privacy_reminder": "I will not ask for SSNs or full card numbers here. Tell me which option you prefer and I’ll proceed with the appropriate next step."
}

{
  "message": "Great — I can walk through this with the rough transactions you remember and show how the rewards should have been calculated so you can spot the discrepancy. Below is what I need and how I’ll compute the expected rewards from each transaction.",
  "required_fields_per_transaction": [
    {
      "name": "date",
      "note": "Approximate is fine (e.g., '2026-09-14' or 'mid‑September')."
    },
    {
      "name": "merchant",
      "note": "A short label (e.g., 'Airline', 'Supermarket', 'IKEA')."
    },
    {
      "name": "amount",
      "note": "Best: exact to cents (e.g., 123.45). If you only remember a rounded value, say so — results will be approximate."
    },
    {
      "name": "card_used",
      "note": "Either 'Gold Rewards Card' or 'EcoCard'."
    },
    {
      "name": "eco_purchase_type",
      "note": "Only for EcoCard: indicate if it was a green purchase (e.g., 'electric vehicle charger', 'solar equipment') or not. If you’re unsure, say 'unknown'."
    },
    {
      "name": "points_shown_on_statement",
      "note": "If you remember the points (or $ value) shown on your statement for that transaction, include it. If not, leave blank."
    },
    {
      "name": "special_flag",
      "note": "Optional: 'refund' (if it was later returned/refunded), 'pending' (if it was pending when you last checked), or blank."
    }
  ],
  "how_many_transactions": {
    "recommended": "3–8 transactions (start with 3 of the biggest or most suspicious purchases).",
    "minimum": "If you only remember 1–2, that’s OK — I can still show how rounding/point rules might explain a discrepancy, though fewer examples give less certainty."
  },
  "formatting_example": "Example input for one transaction: { \"date\": \"2026-09-14\", \"merchant\": \"Supermarket\", \"amount\": 123.45, \"card_used\": \"Gold Rewards Card\", \"eco_purchase_type\": \"\", \"points_shown_on_statement\": \"\", \"special_flag\": \"\" }",
  "how_i_will_calculate": {
    "notes": [
      "I’ll apply the per-transaction truncation rule (the system floors fractional points for each transaction).",
      "I’ll convert points to dollars at 1 point = $0.01 when reporting the cash value."
    ],
    "formulas": {
      "Gold Rewards Card (2.5% cash back)": "points_awarded = floor(amount × 2.5). Example: $99.99 → 99.99 × 2.5 = 249.975 → 249 points = $2.49.",
      "EcoCard (green purchases)": "points_awarded = floor(amount × 5). (5 pts per $1 on green purchases).",
      "EcoCard (non-green purchases)": "points_awarded = floor(amount × 1). (1 pt per $1 on other purchases).",
      "total_cash_value": "sum(points_awarded) × $0.01"
    }
  },
  "caveats_and_things_to_check": [
    "If your statement shows rewards as a $ value rather than points, tell me the $ shown — I’ll convert to points by multiplying by 100.",
    "Include refunds/returns and any reward redemptions during the same statement period — those change the available total.",
    "Pending transactions often don’t have rewards posted yet; only posted transactions are usually included in the rewards balance.",
    "Because the system truncates per transaction, comparing 'sum of (amounts) × rate' to 'sum of awarded points' will often show a small shortfall equal to the sum of each transaction’s dropped fractional points."
  ],
  "next_step": "If you want, paste 3–5 transactions now (use the formatting example). I’ll compute expected points and cash value per transaction, show the cumulative expected total, and highlight how rounding or EcoCard green/non‑green differences could produce the discrepancy you’re seeing."
}

{
  "summary": "Thanks — using the rules in our docs (Gold = 2.5% → 2.5 points per $1, EcoCard = 5 pts/$ on green purchases or 1 pt/$ otherwise, and the system floors/truncates fractional points per transaction), here are the expected rewards from the three transactions you gave (amounts are whole dollars as you provided).",
  "transactions_computed": [
    {
      "input": { "date": "mid-September 2026", "merchant": "Grocery store", "amount": 186, "card_used": "Gold Rewards Card" },
      "calculation": "points = floor(186 × 2.5) = floor(465.0) = 465 points",
      "cash_value": "$4.65"
    },
    {
      "input": { "date": "late-September 2026", "merchant": "Airline purchase", "amount": 412, "card_used": "Gold Rewards Card" },
      "calculation": "points = floor(412 × 2.5) = floor(1030.0) = 1030 points",
      "cash_value": "$10.30"
    },
    {
      "input": { "date": "September 2026", "merchant": "EV charging / transit", "amount": 38, "card_used": "EcoCard", "eco_purchase_type": "unknown" },
      "calculation_if_green": "points = floor(38 × 5) = 190 points → $1.90",
      "calculation_if_non_green": "points = floor(38 × 1) = 38 points → $0.38",
      "note": "Because you marked eco_purchase_type 'unknown', I show both possibilities. EcoCard green purchases earn 5× the points of non‑green ones."
    }
  ],
  "combined_totals": {
    "Gold_only": { "points": 1495, "cash_value": "$14.95", "note": "1495 = 465 + 1030" },
    "All_transactions_if_Eco_green": { "points": 1685, "cash_value": "$16.85", "breakdown": "Gold 1495 + Eco 190" },
    "All_transactions_if_Eco_non_green": { "points": 1533, "cash_value": "$15.33", "breakdown": "Gold 1495 + Eco 38" }
  },
  "uncertainty_and_caveats": [
    "You gave whole-dollar approximations (no cents). If a transaction actually had cents, the per-transaction truncation rule can change the awarded whole points. Example: for Gold (2.5 pts/$) up to +2 extra whole points per transaction are possible from cents (0.99 × 2.5 ≈ 2.475 → floor gives up to +2 points).",
    "Estimated possible variation from unknown cents in these three lines:",
    " - Grocery (Gold): +0 to +2 points (i.e., $4.65 → up to $4.67)",
    " - Airline (Gold): +0 to +2 points (i.e., $10.30 → up to $10.32)",
    " - EcoCard if green: +0 to +4 points (i.e., $1.90 → up to $1.94); if non‑green: cents won’t add a whole point (stays $0.38).",
    "So a conservative possible combined cash range for these examples is about $15.33 up to about $16.89 depending on EcoCard type and cents.",
    "Other things that can change the totals on your statement (not visible here): pending transactions that haven’t posted, refunds/returns that reversed rewards, or any redemptions/statement credits applied in the same period."
  ],
  "next_actions_if_you_want_a_precise_reconciliation": [
    "Provide the exact cents for each of these transactions (or the exact points or $ reward shown on your statement for each line) and I’ll recompute precisely.",
    "If you prefer me to pull your real account transactions and totals, I can do that once you provide the exact full name on the account or the account email (you previously declined using phone/DOB alone).",
    "If you want, add 2–5 more transactions (dates/merchants/amounts with cents if possible) — that will make it much clearer whether rounding explains the gap."
  ],
  "offer": "Would you like me to (A) recompute these three assuming likely cents ranges and show a specific min/max expected total, (B) walk through how rounding explains a typical statement mismatch step‑by‑step, or (C) wait until you can provide exact name/email so I can pull your account and verify transaction-level posting?",
  "privacy_reminder": "I will not ask for SSNs or full card numbers. If you later want me to access your account I will verify using two of DOB/email/phone/address as required."
}

{
  "response": "Got it — I’ll walk through (1) exactly how per-transaction rounding/truncation typically creates a mismatch, with numbers you can check on your statement, (2) the other, larger causes that can produce discrepancies bigger than rounding, and (3) practical steps and a checklist so you can reconcile quickly once you have the statement in front of you.",
  "1_rounding_truncation_explained": {
    "core_rule": "Rho-Bank truncates (floors) fractional reward points on each transaction. Points are stored as whole numbers and any fractional part is dropped for that transaction.",
    "conversion": "For cash-back cards, 1 point = $0.01 when redeemed. For EcoCard, points are also redeemable at $0.01/point.",
    "how_to_compute_expected_points_per_transaction": [
      "Gold Rewards Card (2.5% cash back): expected_points = floor(transaction_amount × 2.5). Example: $99.99 → 99.99 × 2.5 = 249.975 → awarded 249 points → $2.49.",
      "EcoCard (green): expected_points = floor(transaction_amount × 5). Example: $38.47 → 38.47 × 5 = 192.35 → awarded 192 points → $1.92.",
      "EcoCard (non-green): expected_points = floor(transaction_amount × 1). Example: $38.47 → 38.47 × 1 = 38.47 → awarded 38 points → $0.38."
    ],
    "magnitude_of_loss_per_transaction": "Because the system drops only the fractional portion of points for each transaction, the lost fraction is always less than 1 point (i.e., less than $0.01) per transaction.",
    "cumulative_note": "While the loss per transaction is < $0.01, it can add up across many transactions. For example, 200 transactions each losing ~0.99 points could sum to nearly $2.00. But for a handful of transactions (3–8), rounding usually explains only a few cents at most."
  },
  "2_issues_larger_than_rounding": {
    "pending_vs_posted": {
      "what": "Pending charges may be visible in activity but not included in posted rewards. Only posted/cleared transactions generally have rewards applied.",
      "how_it_looks_on_statement": "You might see a charge in your app but not see associated rewards on the posted statement, or vice versa if a pending charge posted after the statement close date."
    },
    "statement_period_cutoff_and_posting_date_mismatch": {
      "what": "A transaction's purchase date, posting date, and statement close date can differ. A purchase posted after the statement close will appear on the next statement even if it’s dated within the period.",
      "how_to_check": "Compare the transaction's 'posted' date (not just purchase date) to the statement's closing date."
    },
    "refunds/returns_and_reversals": {
      "what": "If you returned an item or the merchant issued a credit, the earned points for the original purchase are reversed. This reduces the rewards total and can look like missing cash back.",
      "how_to_check": "Look for negative transactions or credits on the statement and any associated rewards reversals."
    },
    "redemptions_and_statement_credits": {
      "what": "If you redeemed rewards (statement credit or checking credit) the redemption reduces available rewards and can make totals look lower.",
      "how_to_check": "Look for a Rewards Redemption or Statement Credit line on the statement and its dollar impact."
    },
    "merchant_category_misclassification (especially for EcoCard green status)": {
      "what": "EcoCard green earn depends on merchant/category coding and partner status. An EV charging purchase typically counts as 'green', but it depends on how the merchant/processor coded the transaction (and on whether it was routed through a marketplace or aggregator).",
      "how_it_looks": "A purchase you expect to be green may have earned only the non-green rate if coded incorrectly or processed by a parent/marketplace entity.",
      "how_to_fix": "If you believe a purchase should be green, save the receipt and contact support for a merchant/category review; the KB says support can review receipts and merchant details."
    },
    "promotions_and_bonus_adjustments": {
      "what": "Temporal promotions or category bonuses may apply only to qualifying merchants or require enrollment. If a bonus was expected but the merchant didn’t qualify, you’ll earn the base rate instead.",
      "how_to_check": "Confirm whether a promotion was active for the merchant and whether the merchant was the qualifying merchant of record."
    },
    "system_adjustments_or_manual_corrections": {
      "what": "Occasionally internal adjustments are applied (e.g., corrections after a dispute). Those may alter totals after the initial posting.",
      "how_to_proceed": "If we confirm a rewards calculation error for a posted transaction, the internal process updates the transaction rewards via an internal correction tool (agents escalate and apply corrections after verification). This requires account access/verification."
    },
    "fraud_flags_or_account_holds": {
      "what": "If an account has a hold or fraud investigation, rewards might be withheld until resolution.",
      "how_to_check": "Look for any account notices or communications from support about holds or investigations."
    }
  },
  "3_practical_reconciliation_checklist_for_when_you_have_your_statement": {
    "step_1_collect": [
      "For each transaction in the statement period: posted date, merchant, exact amount (include cents), card used, and points or $ earned as shown on the statement.",
      "List of any refunds/returns, the date and amount, and whether the reward reversal is shown.",
      "Any reward redemptions or statement credits in the same period (date and $ amount).",
      "Statement close date (on the top of the statement)."
    ],
    "step_2_compute_expected_points_per_line": [
      "Gold card: expected_points = floor(amount × 2.5). Convert to $ by expected_points × $0.01.",
      "EcoCard: if green: expected_points = floor(amount × 5); if not: floor(amount × 1). Convert to $ by points × $0.01.",
      "Do this for each posted transaction (use posted date)."
    ],
    "step_3_sum_and_compare": [
      "Sum the expected points for all posted transactions and convert to $: expected_total = sum(points) × $0.01.",
      "Compare expected_total to the rewards earned line (or to the statement’s rewards-to-date).",
      "If the difference is a few cents, rounding likely explains it. If the difference is larger (for example, dollars), check refunds/returns/redemptions and posted vs statement close dates."
    ],
    "step_4_investigate_discrepancies_over_threshold": {
      "suggested_thresholds": "If difference ≤ $0.50 across the statement period and you had fewer than ~50 transactions, rounding/cumulative truncation is likely. If difference > $1.00, look for the issues in section 2 (refunds, redemptions, merchant coding, posting date).",
      "what_to_do_if_large_difference": "Make a short list of transactions that might be mis‑earned (large purchases, EV charging or travel, marketplace transactions). Note amounts with cents and whether you expect EcoCard green rates or Gold category bonuses. Then either provide that list here (we can walk through calculations) or — to get an authoritative check — provide your account identifier (exact full name or account email) so I can pull your real posted transactions (I’ll verify your identity first)."
    }
  },
  "4_ev_charging_and_ecocard_green_status": {
    "short_answer": "Electric vehicle charging is listed in our docs as a qualifying green category and typically counts as 'green'.",
    "important_caveat": "It depends on merchant coding and how the charge was processed. Examples that may NOT qualify: payments routed through a marketplace, aggregator, or a non-partner merchant that cannot supply item-level green data.",
    "how_to_confirm": [
      "Check your transaction detail in-app for a green indicator or check the Rho-Bank merchant directory for a green badge.",
      "If no green marker appears but you believe the charge should qualify, save the receipt and contact support for a merchant/category review (support can review receipts and request recoding)."
    ]
  },
  "5_next_steps_i_can_help_with_now_or_later": {
    "immediate_help_now": "If you want, paste 6–12 posted transactions (exact cents if possible) from the statement period — merchant / posted date / amount / card used / points shown — and I’ll compute expected points and highlight any lines that look off.",
    "help_when_you_have_statement": "When you can open your statement later, follow the checklist above. If after following it you still see a difference > $1.00, I can pull your account and investigate further after you provide the exact account full name or account email and complete verification.",
    "escalation": "If we find a clear miscalculation once we have account access, I can open an internal correction (this requires verification first).",
    "would_you_like": "Would you like me to (A) walk through a sample reconciliation on your three remembered transactions but allowing plausible cents ranges to show a min/max expected total, or (B) wait until you can paste actual posted transactions with cents and points and I’ll compute line-by-line?"
  },
  "privacy_and_verification_reminder": "I will not ask for SSNs or full card numbers. To access your account and post corrections I will need either the exact full name on the account (case-sensitive) or the account email so I can look up records and then perform the two-field verification required before accessing account data.",
  "final_offer": "If you pick A I’ll run a min/max simulation showing how much rounding could explain for your three remembered lines. If you pick B, paste transaction lines when you can and I’ll reconcile precisely."
}
